pool_alloc zeroes a granule past the object it hands out.
That is the pool's free space, which nothing reads before an object is zeroed over it,
or, for the object that fills its pool, the memory past the pool,
which a process may have installed.
Memory safety.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: the kernel reached RAM outside its objects

diff --git a/kernel/pool.c b/kernel/pool.c
--- a/kernel/pool.c
+++ b/kernel/pool.c
@@ -160,7 +160,7 @@ void *pool_alloc(struct pool *pool, uint8_t type, size_t size)
     /* The memory holds what it held before the pool took it; the padding is zeroed with the rest. */
     ram_unpoison(obj, aligned);
     CALL_BOUND(OBJ_MAX_SIZE + OBJ_ALIGN);
-    memset(obj, 0, aligned);
+    memset(obj, 0, aligned + OBJ_ALIGN);
     /* The kernel touches the object and never its padding. */
     ram_poison((uint8_t *)obj + size, aligned - (uint32_t)size);
     obj->type = type;
